    {
      "id": "p0024-b009",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "Similarly, there are dozens of Japanese pronouns that mean \"you\" in the singular, but it's just as common to restate the listener's name every time instead of using a pronoun. In fact, it's perfectly normal to drop pronouns entirely in Japanese if it's clear who's being referred to. The Japanese phrase for \"I love you'', ai shiteiru, literally contains no pronouns, for instance.",
        "raw": "Similarly, there are dozens of Japanese pronouns that mean \"you\" in the\nsingular, but it's just as common to restate the listener's name every time\ninstead of using a pronoun. In fact, it's perfectly normal to drop pronouns\nentirely in Japanese if it's clear who's being referred to. The Japanese phrase\nfor \"I love you'', ai shiteiru, literally contains no pronouns, for instance.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.290581,
          0.51966429,
          0.87647267,
          0.60632143
