    {
      "id": "p0024-b012",
      "kind": "paragraph",
      "source": {
        "text": "The same sentence can flow very differently in different languages. For example, English sentences generally go subject-verb-object, as in \"Bob ate a monkey\". But in Japanese, sentences tend to go subject-object-verb, as in \"Bob a monkey ate\". This difference can make a literal translation fall apart when a sentence is incomplete - which is extremely common in Japanese - or when timing is important.",
        "raw": "The same sentence can flow very differently in different languages. For\nexample, English sentences generally go subject-verb-object, as in \"Bob ate a\nmonkey\". But in Japanese, sentences tend to go subject-object-verb, as in \"Bob\na monkey ate\". This difference can make a literal translation fall apart when a\nsentence is incomplete - which is extremely common in Japanese - or when\ntiming is important.",
        "status": "corrected",
        "languages": [
          "en"
        ],
        "bbox": [
          0.29076291,
          0.6949,
          0.89073239,
          0.79909286
        ],
        "mask_bbox": [
          0.28184932,
          0.69418571,
          0.90165709,
          0.80016429
        ],
        "line_ids": [
          39,
          41,
          43,
          45,
          47,
          49
        ]
      },
      "translation": {
        "text": "同一句话在不同语言里的行进顺序可能大不相同。例如英语通常是主语—动词—宾语，像 Bob ate a monkey（Bob 吃了一只猴子）；日语则通常是主语—宾语—动词，相当于“Bob 一只猴子吃了”。遇到不完整的句子——日语里极为常见——或讲究时机的台词，这种差异就可能让直译失效。",
        "status": "draft",
        "spans": []
      },
